What is PWM (Pulse Width Modulation)?
Pulse Width Modulation (PWM) is a technique used to control the amount of power delivered to an electrical device. It does this by rapidly switching a signal between ON and OFF states, varying the width of the "ON" pulse while keeping the frequency constant. The average voltage is determined by the duty cycle (the percentage of time the signal is ON during each cycle).
How PWM Provides an Analog Output
While digital devices can only output HIGH (1) or LOW (0) signals, PWM enables them to simulate an analog output by adjusting the duty cycle. For example, a 50% duty cycle means the signal is HIGH for half the time and LOW for the other half, effectively outputting a voltage that is half of the maximum.
Common applications of PWM include:
  •	Motor speed control
  •	LED brightness adjustment
  •	Signal generation

